WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:06.240
Speaker 1: Let's go next to Joe&nbsp;
from New Hampshire. Joe from New&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:06.240 --> 00:00:09.049
Hampshire. Welcome to the program.
Speaker 3: Hey, can you hear me?&nbsp;

00:00:09.049 --> 00:00:11.520
Speaker 1: Yes, I can.
Speaker 3: Awesome. I have kind of a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:11.520 --> 00:00:18.780
two parter for you. Sure. You know, I'm living&nbsp;
on a college campus right now, and I would say it&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:18.780 --> 00:00:24.180
goes without saying that most people around here&nbsp;
are more political than the average population.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:24.180 --> 00:00:28.980
Sure. But even then, I mean, probably the majority&nbsp;
of people, I mean, are would consider themselves&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:28.980 --> 00:00:31.920
apolitical. They don't like to touch politics.
Speaker 1: Interesting.&nbsp;

00:00:33.060 --> 00:00:37.260
Speaker 3: And I'm noticing a lot of people I&nbsp;
mean, just in general seem to think that like&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:37.260 --> 00:00:42.300
being apolitical is some kind of virtue. Hmm.
Speaker 1: Yeah, It's similar to enlightened&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:42.300 --> 00:00:45.360
centrism. There are people who believe that&nbsp;
because they're in the middle and they're like,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:45.360 --> 00:00:50.220
Well, listen, you know, one side wants to kill&nbsp;
transpeople and the other wants them to be able&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:50.220 --> 00:00:54.720
to use whatever bathroom they want. I'm in the&nbsp;
middle. It's like you're in the middle of one&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:54.720 --> 00:00:59.220
position. That's crazy, dude. You know? So I think&nbsp;
the enlightened centrism is similar in a way,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:00.240 --> 00:01:03.960
but I also don't want to. Now, let me say&nbsp;
one thing here, Joe, before you move on.&nbsp;

00:01:04.740 --> 00:01:09.000
Speaker 3: Right.
Speaker 1: I don't think that political apathy&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:09.540 --> 00:01:17.700
is a good thing because political apathy, carried&nbsp;
to its full conclusion, allows small groups of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:17.700 --> 00:01:22.560
extremists to basically decide how our entire&nbsp;
political system works. So I think knowing what's&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:22.560 --> 00:01:28.440
going on and voting very, very important, at the&nbsp;
risk of turning people off from my own program,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:28.440 --> 00:01:37.740
I do think there are some people who are too&nbsp;
sucked in to political issues and events and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:37.740 --> 00:01:43.320
news that will never affect them and that they&nbsp;
will never affect. And there are people who&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:43.320 --> 00:01:47.460
have written about this. It's not nihilism,&nbsp;
but it's sort of understanding, you know,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:47.460 --> 00:01:54.480
okay, what am I going to do about what's&nbsp;
going on in Ukraine or the South China Sea&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:54.480 --> 00:01:59.520
or Bubba blah, blah, blah. And I've seen for&nbsp;
some people it becomes unhealthy where they're&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:59.520 --> 00:02:04.680
so sucked into news and politics, they're just&nbsp;
upset. They're not doing and on a lot of things,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:04.680 --> 00:02:08.460
they can't do anything about it other than I might&nbsp;
occasionally donate to a cause or vote or whatever&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:08.460 --> 00:02:13.440
the case may be. And so I do think it's important&nbsp;
and I do this myself. I think shows like mine,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:13.440 --> 00:02:18.360
if you imagine like a pyramid instead of a food&nbsp;
pyramid, it's like an information pyramid. I&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:18.360 --> 00:02:22.080
should be at the top of the pyramid, sort of&nbsp;
like the junk food. You're using this to fill&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:22.080 --> 00:02:28.380
in your understanding of news and events built&nbsp;
on an understanding of history and other areas&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:28.380 --> 00:02:34.200
built on a foundation of critical thinking&nbsp;
and epistemology. And there are some people&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:34.200 --> 00:02:38.100
who are just constantly consuming opinion. And&nbsp;
I don't think that that's a good thing to do.&nbsp;

00:02:38.100 --> 00:02:44.280
Speaker 3: Yeah, absolutely. I definitely agree.&nbsp;
I think my main concern is that there's a lot of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:44.280 --> 00:02:50.340
people who, for instance, in my state in New&nbsp;
Hampshire, we elected to Democratic senators,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:50.340 --> 00:02:57.420
but we reelected the the anti-abortion&nbsp;
Republican Republican governor. Yes. And&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:57.420 --> 00:03:02.520
I was talking with some people after after I&nbsp;
personally voted and they were talking about&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:02.520 --> 00:03:06.120
who they voted for. And they're like, yeah,&nbsp;
you know, I'm not too political. So. But,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:06.120 --> 00:03:10.860
you know, I still like abortion. So I voted&nbsp;
for the two Democratic senators and the and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:10.860 --> 00:03:14.820
Chris Sununu because they thought Chris Sununu&nbsp;
was pro-abortion. Right. And they just, like,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:14.820 --> 00:03:19.500
were not critical of who they were voting for.
Speaker 1: Yes. No, that's a real problem. And&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:19.500 --> 00:03:24.420
I also know people who are like, yeah, I'm not&nbsp;
really political, but I just am kind of more&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:24.420 --> 00:03:28.320
of a Republican. So I don't get very involved or&nbsp;
pay attention, but I just vote for Republicans.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:28.320 --> 00:03:32.460
But I'm not really very political. And then&nbsp;
I talk to the person and all of their views&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:32.460 --> 00:03:37.920
actually really align more with the Democratic&nbsp;
Party. So sometimes that apathy is actually more&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:37.920 --> 00:03:41.940
destructive than people who are involved.
Speaker 3: Yeah, that's. That's&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:41.940 --> 00:03:46.260
kind of what I was thinking, too. Yeah.
Speaker 1: Well, Joe, I appreciate your breaking&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:46.260 --> 00:03:49.489
bringing up the issue. It's an important one.
Speaker 3: Yeah. Thanks for taking my call.&nbsp;

00:03:49.489 --> 00:03:52.260
Speaker 1: All right. There is Joe&nbsp;
from New Hampshire. Great to hear from.

